# Original IVT examples form a parallel twelve-item application bank.
D[3]['worked']=normalize([
example('1 · A polynomial root in a specified interval',r'Prove x³+x−1 has a root in (0,1).',r'Its polynomial left side is continuous on [0,1]; its endpoint values are −1 and 1. IVT supplies an interior zero. We have proved existence, not uniqueness.'),
example('2 · A transcendental crossing',r'Prove cos x=x has a solution in (0,1).',r'Set h(x)=cos x−x. It is continuous, h(0)=1>0, and h(1)=cos1−1<0. IVT gives a zero of h, which is the original equality. The useful function was the difference.'),
example('3 · Every target value of an odd polynomial',r'For any real k, show x³+x=k has a real solution.',r'Fix k and put h(x)=x³+x−k. Choose R>|k|+1. Then h(R)>0 and h(−R)<0. Continuity gives a zero between −R and R. The interval may depend on the chosen target k.'),
example('4 · A fixed point of a self-map',r'If continuous f maps [0,1] into [0,1], prove f(c)=c somewhere.',r'Use h(x)=f(x)−x. Its endpoint values satisfy h(0)≥0 and h(1)≤0. If an endpoint is zero we are done; otherwise strict opposite signs give an interior zero. A non-strict sign condition allows an endpoint solution.'),
example('5 · Two roots require two brackets',r'Prove x²+x−1 has two distinct real zeros using IVT.',r'The values at −2,0,1 are 1,−1,1. Continuity yields one root in (−2,0) and another in (0,1). The disjoint intervals ensure distinctness. No conclusion about “exactly two” follows from these two IVT applications alone; the quadratic degree can supply an upper bound.'),
example('6 · A parameter family without solving it',r'For every real a, prove x³+ax−1 has a real root.',r'Fix a and choose R>|a|+2. Then R³−|a|R−1>0. Hence h(R)>0 and h(−R)<0 for h(x)=x³+ax−1. The polynomial is continuous, so some zero lies between them. Explicit algebraic root formulas are unnecessary.'),
example('7 · Bisection retains a certified root',r'Bracket a root of x³+x−1 on [0,1], then take one bisection.',r'The endpoint signs are opposite. At 1/2 the value is 1/8+1/2−1=−3/8. The retained interval [1/2,1] still has opposite signs and continuity, so IVT certifies a root there. The midpoint decision is based on sign, not on which endpoint looks visually nearer.'),
example('8 · Equal endpoint values and a shifted argument',r'If continuous f on [0,2] satisfies f(0)=f(2), prove some c∈[0,1] has f(c)=f(c+1).',r'Let h(x)=f(x)−f(x+1) on [0,1]. Then h(0)=f(0)−f(1) and h(1)=f(1)−f(2)=−h(0). If these are zero, an endpoint works; otherwise use IVT. The auxiliary function exposes opposite signs that were not visible in f alone.'),
example('9 · Equal temperatures at opposite points',r'A temperature T is continuous around a circle. Show there are opposite points with equal temperature.',r'Parameterize the circle with a continuous 2π-periodic T(θ). Let h(θ)=T(θ)−T(θ+π). Then h(π)=−h(0). IVT on [0,π] gives a zero, including an endpoint if applicable. This is a one-dimensional argument, not a claim about discontinuous temperature assignments.'),
example('10 · Averages of a continuous function are attained',r'If f is continuous on [a,b], a<b, show some c has f(c)=(1/(b−a))∫ₐᵇf(x)dx.',r'Continuity on a compact interval gives a minimum m and maximum M. Integrating m≤f≤M and dividing by b−a puts the average between m and M. IVT then makes f attain that average somewhere. This uses the extreme value theorem and integral bounds as well as IVT; it is not the derivative MVT.'),
example('11 · A root missed by endpoint sign change',r'Why does f(x)=x² on [−1,1] show that opposite endpoint signs are not necessary for a root?',r'Both endpoint values are 1, yet f(0)=0. Opposite signs plus continuity are sufficient. The converse “no sign change, no zero” is false. This example trains the direction of the implication.'),
example('12 · Failure when the graph can jump',r'Why do the endpoint values of 1/x on [−1,1] not prove a zero?',r'The function is undefined at zero and is not continuous on the interval. It takes values −1 and 1 at the endpoints but never equals zero. The missing hypothesis is precisely what would have prevented jumping across the target.')])
D[3]['problems']=D[3]['problems'][:5]
D[3]['story']+= '<p>The following twelve examples are an original IVT application ladder. For each, identify the continuous function, the interval, the target, and the endpoint information before reading the argument. Examples 8–10 are extensions. The aim is to learn how to construct the function to which IVT applies.</p>'
