mvt=normalize([
example('1 · Why the theorem matters',r'If f is differentiable on R and f′(x)=0 everywhere, can f(0) and f(1) differ?',r'MVT on [0,1] gives f(1)−f(0)=f′(c)·1=0. The derivative contains local slope information; the theorem translates it into a statement about two separated values. Differentiability on an interval is crucial.'),
example('2 · Local extrema need not characterize zero derivatives',r'Compare f(x)=x², g(x)=x³, and h(x)=|x| at zero.',r'x² has a local minimum and derivative zero. x³ has derivative zero but no local extremum. |x| has a local minimum but no derivative. The theorem says a differentiable interior local extremum has derivative zero; it does not reverse that implication or remove differentiability.'),
example('3 · Why an interior extremum has zero derivative',r'Let differentiable f have a local maximum at c. Why is f′(c)=0?',r'For small h>0, [f(c+h)−f(c)]/h≤0. For small h<0, the same nonpositive numerator divided by a negative number gives a quotient ≥0. If the two-sided derivative exists, it must be both ≤0 and ≥0, hence zero. At an interval endpoint there may be only one allowed side.'),
example('4 · Find the actual global extrema',r'Find the extrema of x³−3x on [−2,2].',r'The function is continuous on a compact interval, so extrema exist. Interior critical points solve 3x²−3=0, giving ±1. Check those and endpoints: f(−2)=−2, f(−1)=2, f(1)=−2, f(2)=2. Thus maximum 2 at −1 and 2; minimum −2 at −2 and 1. Critical points alone would miss endpoint ties.'),
example('5 · Rolle’s theorem',r'Find c for f(x)=x²−4 on [−2,2].',r'Continuity and differentiability hold, and endpoint values agree at zero. Rolle gives an interior zero derivative. Since f′=2x, that point is c=0. The equal endpoints, not zeros specifically, are the relevant condition.'),
example('6 · Count zeros by counting possible turns',r'Prove x³−3x+4 has exactly one real zero.',r'The values at −3 and −2 are −14 and 2, so IVT gives a root in (−3,−2). The derivative is 3(x²−1), positive for x<−1, negative on (−1,1), positive for x>1. At the turning points the values are 6 and 2. Hence the function is positive for x≥−1 and strictly increasing on (−∞,−1), so the bracketed root is unique. Rolle also says two roots would force a derivative zero between them; it supplies upper-bound logic, while IVT supplies existence.'),
example('7 · Locate the MVT point',r'For f(x)=x² on [1,3], find the tangent slope promised by MVT.',r'The chord slope is (9−1)/(3−1)=4. Solve f′(c)=2c=4, giving c=2 inside the interval. The theorem first guarantees such a c from its hypotheses; solving the equation then identifies it.'),
example('8 · Prove MVT by subtracting the chord',r'How does Rolle’s theorem prove MVT for f on [a,b]?',r'Let m=[f(b)−f(a)]/(b−a) and h(x)=f(x)−f(a)−m(x−a). This subtracts the chord, making h(a)=h(b)=0. It preserves continuity and interior differentiability. Rolle gives h′(c)=0; since h′=f′−m, we obtain f′(c)=m. The auxiliary function was designed to match the theorem’s endpoint hypothesis.'),
example('9 · Zero derivative and a disconnected-domain trap',r'Why can 1 for x>0 and −1 for x<0 have derivative zero without being one constant?',r'The domain is R without zero, which is not an interval. MVT proves constancy separately on each interval (−∞,0) and (0,∞), but cannot be applied between a negative and a positive input across the missing point.'),
example('10 · Why antiderivatives differ only by a constant',r'If F′=f and G′=f on the same interval, show the relation between F and G.',r'For H=F−G, H′=0. MVT between any two points makes H constant, so F=G+C on that interval. This explains the +C in indefinite integrals. It does not, on its own, prove that every function possesses an antiderivative.'),
example('11 · From derivative sign to monotonicity',r'Prove x+arctan x is strictly increasing on R.',r'Its derivative is 1+1/(1+x²)>0. Given any a<b, MVT gives f(b)−f(a)=f′(c)(b−a)>0. This quantifies why a local slope condition controls all pairs of inputs on the interval.'),
example('12 · An interval sign chart',r'Find intervals of increase and decrease of f(x)=x³−3x.',r'The derivative is 3(x−1)(x+1). It is positive on (−∞,−1), negative on (−1,1), and positive on (1,∞). MVT converts those signs into increasing, decreasing, increasing intervals. At −1 there is a local maximum; at 1 a local minimum. Zero derivative at a boundary point does not defeat strict increase on an adjacent interval.')])
